From: tribble@memex.com (E. Dean Tribble)
Subject: Traffic guide

	 One aspect that I find interesting (from the "Selfish Gene" and
	 "spontaneous order" points of view) is the interaction of the
	 self-interest of the driver with the "collective interest" of getting
	 the most traffic through.  In many cases, a driver is forbidden from
	 doing something that would be in his self-interest, in order to make
	 the overall traffic flow work better.  For instance, stopping at
	 traffic lights.  (Classic Tragedy of the Commons.)  In this case,
	 "altruism" can be enforced, because violations are obvious.

This is much more analogous to the iterated prisoner's dilemna rather
than just the prisoner's dilemna.  In the single case it is in the
driver's self-interest to ignore the rules, but in the iterated case
(where many drivers are potentially ignoring the rules many times) it
quickly becomes in everyone's interest to obey the rules.  The
incentives change dramatically in the iterated case.

dean


