Tom Litow comments on Derek Zahn's question: (sorry for quoting this
in its entirety, but the material is by nature confusing)

> derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn) writes:
> 
> >>  Suppose I have a machine that executes the following operation:
> >>  Register A := Register A + Register B.  Now, supposedly this
> >>  sort of operation must pay a penalty for erasing the bits in
> >>  Register A.  But entropy as usually presented is a measure of
> >>  uncertainty, which would imply that the uncertainty about the
> >>  machine state has supposedly gone down (thus I must pay that
> >>  back somehow with heat or some other disordering process).  But
> >>  surely it hasn't!  I knew what the machine's state was before,
> >>  and I know it now.  If anything, I'm less sure because it could
> >>  have malfunctioned.  Put another way, there is no information
> >>  gain involved here, whether rubegoldbergian cascade-type mechanisms
> >>  are in place or not.  What gives?
> 
> 
> Interesting question.  My guess is that the confusion comes from not
> precisely defining what the "system" is.  You're correct in that the
> uncertainty of Register A's contents has not changed, but the system
> also contains wires, etc, whose atoms you disturbed in unpredictable
> ways by performing the computation.  Therefore, the heat produced is
> from the work done by the electric current through a resistive medium,
> and I would expect the total entropy of the computational system to 
> thereby increase - "caused" by the electric current, _not_ any change
> in uncertainty in our knowledge of the register contents.
> 
> Tom Litow (ex-physics person corrupted by software)

Nope, your guess is wrong. Or at least it is only partly right, in the
sense that a reduction in information entropy implies an increase in
physical entropy (phase space cannot be compressed in a conservative
system). The effect Derek is referring to--and questioning--is much
more basic and subtle than stuff about resistive heating of wires...it
is basic to the nature of computation.

If indeed the information about the states prior to a computation is
recorded (written down, stored in the system), then a reversible
computation is possible with arbitrarily small energy dissipation. The
"billiard ball" computers of Fredkin and others show this.

Me, I stay away from making entropy arguments based on intuitive ideas
about what is "uncertain" and what "reduces uncertainty." These are
very slippery concepts--witness the constantly recurring debate about
whether "noise" has low entropy ("carries no information") or high
entropy ("maximally uncertain").

I recommend the books "Maxwell's Demon: Entropy, Information, and
Computing," Leff and Rex, "Complexity, Entropy and the Physics of
Information," Zurek, and "Proceedings of the Workshop on Physics and
Computation," 1992.

-Tim May


