   This often confuses me too because I haven't learned information
theory yet or statistcal mechanics. However, Robin Hanson gave
a pretty intuitive model in his physics of computation paper.

  Consider a gate which takes three inputs and produces 1 output.
Where did the energy from the other two inputs go? Since the inputs and
output are restricted to two states, the energy couldn't have gone into
the strength of the output signal, therefore some of it must have been
given off as heat because of conservation of energy. If my recollection
is correct, this shows that heat is released when information is destroyed.
Not a proof, but it is an intuitive way to picture the concept without
resorting to phase space, statistics, and all that other junk. ;-)




