Mike Wiik writes:
> 
> Ray writes:
> >   Consider a gate which takes three inputs and produces 1 output.
> > Where did the energy from the other two inputs go? Since the inputs and
> > output are restricted to two states, the energy couldn't have gone into
> > the strength of the output signal, therefore some of it must have been
> > given off as heat because of conservation of energy. If my recollection
> > is correct, this shows that heat is released when information is destroyed.
> 
> How is this different from burning a book to get warm? If the output
> randomly selected from one of the 3 inputs, how does this affect your
> model?

  It doesn't, the concept boils down to this. You've got a signal
which takes some form of energy to transmit, whether it is light, 
electrons, or kinetic energy in rods.  Signals can be either H "high", or L
"low". Three of these signals, I1, I2, and I3 enter a blackbox. The blackbox
can combine these signals anyway it wants (even picking one at random)
but the output must be a single signal O and that is also restricted to
either H or L.

 Let's arbitrarily set "low" to mean 1 foobar unit, and "high" to 2 foobar
units.

   Remember, that the E (energy) of the system before the blackbox
equals the energy after.

 Consider this possibility:

I1 = 1
I2 = 1
I3 = 1
(all low) total energy, 3 foobars input (lowest possible input energy)

The most the blackbox can output is 2 foobars, so if three foobars
are input and two are output, where did the other go? 

  The only way I see to get out of this situation is for our blackbox
to store the "lost energy" instead of dissipating it and release it
later when the operation is "reversed". I don't know whether this is 
possible though.

  Robin Hanson in his reversible agents paper states it like this:
[hope he doesn't mind me quoting him]
"...  That is, knowing the exact state of the universe at one
moment in time in principle tells you enough to deduce its state at any
future or past moment in time; the mapping of states between times is
one to one.  A system known to be in one of ten possible states at one
time is in principle known to be in one of ten states at all other
times.  Therefore if one wants to create a physical device which maps
many possible initial states onto a few possible final states, one must
pay for this somewhere else in the universe by mapping a few initial
states onto many possible final states there.  This is the only way the
total mapping can stay one to one."

  Robin's version is different and more abstract. I was trying to think
up a real world example. If I interpret robin's statement correctly,
then the one-to-many mapping is what we call entropy increase, disorder,
thermal noise, heat dissipation, etc. 



-Ray


