> I can set a bit to zero, and I can set a bit to one, how to I set it
> to "erase"? Seems like a trinary system.
> 
>  Let's say you are about to store a zero bit in Josh's rod logic
> model, but the bit in question is already zero! Does this require
> energy? Do you have to erase a bit before you write to it?

Drexler's rods in Nanosystems are normally in one state or the other,
although during the write cycle they go through a transition state,
of course.

I do't think "erasure" is the most useful concept for these rods;
rather, any write operation (whether a 0 or a 1) will cost energy
because you have forgotten what value was held before.  It is the act
of forgetting which causes the energy cost.  The operation is irreversible:
0->0  and  1->0  would be the state transitions for writing a zero.

Now, theoretically, if you could deduce what had been there before even
after you have written the zero (like, if it was an earlier result of
the calculation which can be deduced from the current global state of
your computer), then you haven't lost information and in principle the
0 could be stored without costing energy.

But I think you'd have to use some other method to store your 0 in this
case than Drexler's simple gates.  His gates thermalize the energy lost
as the store occurs, and that heat can't be recaptured.

